WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.432
Start with the property the application
needs.

2
00:00:03.432 --> 00:00:05.290
Confidentiality, integrity, authenticity,
and

3
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.440
password verification require appropriate
constructions.

4
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.884
Encoding changes representation, while
encryption protects

5
00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:12.034
recoverable data with a key.

6
00:00:12.034 --> 00:00:14.380
Ordinary hashing does not provide
encryption

7
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.313
or an appropriate password verifier by
itself.

8
00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:19.854
Use maintained, high level cryptographic
interfaces,

9
00:00:19.854 --> 00:00:22.591
and prefer authenticated encryption when
stored data

10
00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:25.133
needs both confidentiality and tamper
detection.

11
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:29.238
Keep authorization and TLS as separate
parts of the system.

12
00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:32.562
Generate keys and security values with
cryptographic randomness.

13
00:00:32.562 --> 00:00:35.006
Follow the exact nonce contract, including

14
00:00:35.006 --> 00:00:37.938
uniqueness under the same key where
required.

15
00:00:37.938 --> 00:00:41.164
A nonce is not a secret key, and

16
00:00:41.164 --> 00:00:44.878
random generation does not eliminate
collision or operational limits.

17
00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.224
Human passwords need deliberately
expensive password

18
00:00:47.224 --> 00:00:49.375
hashing or password based derivation.

19
00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:51.721
Salts normally accompany the stored result

20
00:00:51.721 --> 00:00:54.262
and are not secret encryption keys.

21
00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:56.999
Derive subkeys from strong material only
with

22
00:00:56.999 --> 00:00:59.932
an appropriate construction and explicit
purpose separation.

23
00:00:59.932 --> 00:01:03.158
A data encryption key protects application
content; a

24
00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.090
key encryption key protects the data key.

25
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.609
Envelope encryption can keep long lived
keys in managed

26
00:01:09.609 --> 00:01:13.324
infrastructure while allowing scoped data
keys and controlled rewrapping.

27
00:01:13.324 --> 00:01:16.061
It does not prevent plaintext data keys

28
00:01:16.061 --> 00:01:18.993
from entering application memory during
local operations.

29
00:01:18.993 --> 00:01:21.535
Minimize that exposure, separate
environments, and

30
00:01:21.535 --> 00:01:24.467
make key access narrowly authorized and
auditable.

31
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:26.813
Type declarations and delivery through
configuration

32
00:01:26.813 --> 00:01:28.964
do not supply those controls.

33
00:01:28.964 --> 00:01:31.310
Preserve controlled format and key version

34
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.851
metadata so historical records remain
understandable.

35
00:01:33.851 --> 00:01:37.761
New write rotation, re-encryption, and
rewrapping perform different jobs.

36
00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:40.107
Emergency compromise response must address
exposure

37
00:01:40.107 --> 00:01:42.258
that routine rotation cannot undo.

38
00:01:42.258 --> 00:01:45.679
Test authentication failures, malformed
records, nonce allocation, retired

39
00:01:45.679 --> 00:01:49.491
key policy, and migration before depending
on those paths.

40
00:01:49.491 --> 00:01:53.597
A cryptographic design is not complete
when encryption works today.

41
00:01:53.597 --> 00:01:57.116
It is complete when the application can
use the

42
00:01:57.116 --> 00:02:00.928
right primitive safely, protect its keys,
detect tampering, rotate

43
00:02:00.928 --> 00:02:04.740
those keys, and still understand protected
data years later.

